  Exercise E.3 - Multi-Node NCCL Tuning (ring vs tree model)
======================================================================

-- Section 1: Ring AllReduce Bandwidth --

  Ring AllReduce moves 2*(N-1)/N * S bytes per GPU and takes 2*(N-1) steps.
  Small messages are dominated by per-step latency; large messages approach
  the link's streaming bandwidth (the 'bus bandwidth').

       msg    ring us   bus GB/s
        1K      150.0       0.01
       64K      152.5       0.81
        1M      189.3      10.38
       64M     2666.6      47.19
     1024M    40415.3      49.81
  [check] large messages approach link bandwidth (bw-bound)

-- Section 2: Tree vs Ring --

  A tree AllReduce completes in ~2*log2(N) steps instead of 2*(N-1). For small,
  latency-bound messages that is a large win; for big messages ring's higher
  bandwidth utilization wins. NCCL_ALGO lets you force one or the other.

       msg    ring us    tree us   winner
        1K      150.0       40.0     tree
        8K      150.3       40.3     tree
       64K      152.5       42.6     tree
      512K      169.7       61.0     tree
        4M      307.3      207.8     tree
       64M     2666.6     2724.4     ring
  Crossover near        : ~65536K bytes
  [check] tree wins small (latency), ring wins large (bandwidth)

-- Section 3: The Node-Boundary Cliff --

  Inside a node, NVLink (~600 GB/s) carries the collective; once it crosses to a
  second node over InfiniBand (~50 GB/s) the same AllReduce gets ~10x slower.
  That step-down is the efficiency cliff you see going from 8 to 16 GPUs.

  8 GPU  (NVLink 600 GB/s) :    3.16 ms
  16 GPU (IB     50 GB/s)  :   40.42 ms  (13x slower)
  [check] the node boundary, not GPU count, causes the cliff

